#---
# purpose: "two polymorphic @spec clauses that between them accept every member of a union argument accept the call: arrow-set coverage reads a var-carrying clause domain at any"
#---
#
# Regression target for fz-kdt.192. A parameter position observes its
# ARGUMENT, so no single clause of `pick/1` accepts `{binary, int} | {int, int}`:
# `{integer, a}` rejects the `{binary, int}` member and `{binary, a}` rejects
# the `{int, int}` member. Every runtime value of that argument type is still
# accepted by exactly one clause, so the call is legal, and the only thing that
# can see it is arrow-SET coverage over the clause set.
#
# A clause variable still free after its bounds are closed accepts anything, so
# the domain of `pick({integer, a})` is `{integer, any}`. Skipping var-carrying
# rows instead reported no coverage where coverage holds, and this program --
# which has always run -- died with error[spec/violation] on all three paths.
defmodule M do
  @spec pick({integer, a}) :: a
  @spec pick({binary, a}) :: a
  fn pick({_tag, v}), do: v
end

fn main() do
  xs = [{1, 7}, {"s", 8}]
  [h | _] = xs
  dbg(M.pick(h))
end
